hen\nG{1,2c}(On) = G{1,2c}(2ac + 1) ⊕G{1,2c}(4c + 2)\n= G{1,6}(6a + 1) ⊕G{1,6}(12 + 2) = G{1,6}(O′\nn′).\nThe first and last equalities are by definition, and the middle one will be by assuming the induction hypothesis on n from\nTheorem 15. Lemma 17(c) then verifies that the resulting option O′\nn′ corresponds to On via φ2. In other words, that the\nthird equality of the following holds:\nn′ = (6a + 1) + (12 + 2) = φ1(2ac + 1) + φ1(4c + 2) = φ2(2ac + 1 + 4c + 2) = φ2(n)\nProof of Lemma 16. Let n = k + p −1. As we saw in our examples above, this holds trivially if n < p. Suppose n ≥p,\nand toward an induction that for all n′ < n, both Lemma 16 and Theorem 15 hold.\nCase 1: 2 ≤p ≤3 In order to show N(n, p, {1, 2c}) ⊆N(φp(n), p, {1, 6}), let On = (h1, . . . , hp) be a partition of n with p\nparts. If On is either an entering or exiting partition, then\nG{1,2c}(On) = G{1,2c}(h1) ⊕· · · ⊕G{1,2c}(hp)\nDefinition 2\n= G{1,6}(φ1(h1)) ⊕· · · ⊕G{1,6}(φ1(hp))\ninduction on n\n= G{1,6}(φ1(h1), . . . , φ1(hp))\nDefinition 2\n∈N(φ1(h1) + · · · + φ1(hp), p, {1, 6})\nDefinition 5\n= N(φp(h1 + · · · + hp), p, {1, 6})\nLemma 17(c)\n= N(φp(n), p, {1, 6})\nIf On is an intermediate partition, then consider its floor ⌊On⌋and ceiling ⌈On⌉, partitions on a, b respectively, with\na < n < b. By the above calculation, G{1,2c}(⌊On⌋) ∈N(φp(a), p, {1, 6}), G{1,2c}(⌈On⌉) ∈N(φp(b), p, {1, 6}), and G{1,2c}(On) ∈\nN(φ1(h1) + · · · + φ1(hp), p, {1, 6}). Furthermore,\nφp(a) = φ1(⌊h1⌋) + · · · + φ1(⌊\nhp\n⌋\n) ≤φ1(h1) + · · · + φ1(hp)\n≤φ1(⌈h1⌉) + · · · + φ1(⌈\nhp\n⌉\n) = φp(b)\nwhere the inequalities are Lemma 17(d) and the equalities are Lemma 17(c). Let Q be a partition of φ1(h1) + · · · + φ1(hp)\nwith p parts satisfying G{1,6}(Q ) = G{1,2c}(On). By Lemma 17(a), φp(n) has the same parity as φ1(h1) + · · · + φ1(hp), and\nLemma 17(d) implies φp(a) ≤φp(n) ≤φp(b), so by Corollary 9, there is a partition R of φp(n) with p parts satisfying\nG{1,6}(R) = G{1,6}(Q ). Hence G{1,2c}(On) ∈N(φp(n), p, {1, 6}).\nWe still need to show N(φp(n), p, {1, 6}) ⊆N(n, p, {1, 2c}). First notice that the map φp is surjective on the set of\nall partitions. Thus we will be done if we can show φp(n) = φp(n′) H⇒\nN(n, p, {1, 2c}) = N(n′, p, {1, 2c}). Examining\nTables 5 and 6, we see that we only need to show the following.\n400--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nDiscrete Mathematics Letters (https://www.dmlett.com/archive/v12/DML23_v12_pp209-216.pdf)\nciteturn6search13 [wordlim: 200] Published: 6 months ago; We investigate the Sprague-Grundy sequences for two normal-play impartial games based on arithmetic functions, ﬁrst ... function.\nKeywords: combinatorial game theory; arithmetic functions; experimental mathematics.\nDiscrete Mathematics Letters\nwww.dmlett.com\nDiscrete Math. Lett. 12 (2023) 209–216\nDOI: 10.47443/dml.2023.154\nResearch Article\nTwo games on arithmetic functions: SALIQUANT and NONTOTIENT\nPaul Ellis1,∗, Jason Shi2, Thotsaporn Aek Thanatipanonda3, Andrew Tu4\n1Department of Mathematics, Rutgers University, 110 Frelinghuysen Road, Piscataway, NJ, 08854, USA\n2California Institute of Technology, 1200 E California Blvd, Pasadena, CA, 91125, USA\n3Science Division, Mahidol University International College, Salaya, Phutthamonthon District, Nakhon Pathom, 73170, Thailand\n4Brunswick School, 100 Maher Ave, Greenwich, CT, 06830, USA\n(Received: 31 August 2023. Received in revised form: 16 December 2023. Accepted: 22 December 2023. Published online: 31 December 2023.)\n© 2023 the authors. This is an open-access article under the CC BY (International 4.0) license (www.creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/).\nAbstract\nWe investigate the Sprague-Grundy sequences for two normal-play impartial games based on arithmetic functions, ﬁrst\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nPaul Ellis (https://www.paulellis.org/)\nciteturn6search0 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: 3 weeks ago; Two Games on Arithmetic Functions: SALIQUANT and NONTOTIENT ... The game CUT is arithmetic-periodic for C={1,2c}\n\nwith Rhett Barton and Samuel Coskey\n\nArt Discrete Appl. Math. 9 (2026), #3.08\n\nhttps://doi.org/10.26493/2590-9770.1621.bf9\n\n12. The Penults of Tak: Adventures in impartial, normal-play, positional games\n\nwith Boris Alexeev, Michael Richter, and Thotsaporn Thanatipanonda\n\nIntegers (2025), 18pp.\n\nhttps://doi.org/10.5281/zenodo.15283760\n\n11. Categories of impartial rulegraphs and gamegraphs\n\nwith Bojan Basic, Dana C. Ernst, Danijela Popovic, and Nandor Sieben\n\nInt J Game Theory (2024). Volume 53,1407–1433.\n\nhttps://doi.org/10.1007/s00182-024-00921-3\n\n1 0. Two Games on Arithmetic Functions: SALIQUANT and NONTOTIENT\n\nwith Jason Shi, Thotsaporn Thanatipanonda, and Andrew Tu\n\nDiscrete Math. Lett. 12 (2023) 209–216. \n\nhttps://doi.org/10.47443/dml.2023.154\n\n9. The game CUT is arithmetic-periodic for C={1,2c}\n\nwith Thotsaporn Aek Thanatipanonda\n\nDiscrete Applied Mathematics, Volume 322 (2022), 391-403. \n\nhttps://doi.org/10.1016/j.dam.2022.08.027\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nTwo Games on Arithmetic Functions: SALIQUANT and NONTOTIENT - arXiv Combinatorics (https://arxiv.symmetricfunctions.com/paper/2309.01231v1)\nciteturn6search1 [wordlim: 200] Published: 3.2 years ago; Crawled: last month; arithmetic function sequence\n\n# • BIB • PDF • ARXIV • ... We investigate the Sprague-Grundy sequences for two normal-play impartial games based on arithmetic functions, first described by Iannucci and Larsson in \\cite{sum}. In each game, the set of positions is N (natural numbers).\n\n## Two Games on Arithmetic Functions: SALIQUANT and NONTOTIENT\n\nPaul Ellis, Jason Shi, Thotsaporn Aek Thanatipanonda, Andrew Tu\n\nPublished: 2023-09-03\n\nComments: 8 pages, 1 figure\n\nNote:\n\nPublication:\n\narithmetic function sequence\n\n# • BIB • PDF • ARXIV •\n\n### Abstract\n\nWe investigate the Sprague-Grundy sequences for two normal-play impartial games based on arithmetic functions, first described by Iannucci and Larsson in \\cite{sum}. In each game, the set of positions is N (natural numbers). In saliquant, the options are to subtract a non-divisor. Here we obtain several nice number theoretic lemmas, a fundamental theorem, and two conjectures about the eventual density of Sprague-Grundy values. In nontotient, the only option is to subtract the number of relatively prime residues. Here are able to calculate certain Sprague-Grundy values, and start to understand an appropriate class function.--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nTwo games on arithmetic functions: SALIQUANT and NONTOTIENT | Request PDF (https://www.researchgate.net/publication/402708815_Two_games_on_arithmetic_functions_SALIQUANT_and_NONTOTIENT)\nciteturn6search2 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: 4 months ago; # Two games on arithmetic functions: SALIQUANT and NONTOTIENT ... Image: Request Full-text Paper PDF ... Lessons in Play: An Introduction to Combinatorial Game Theory ... AN ARITHMETIC FUNCTION ARISING FROM THE 谩 FUNCTION\n\n# Two games on arithmetic functions: SALIQUANT and NONTOTIENT\n\n  * December 2023\n  * Discrete Mathematics Letters 12\n\nDOI:10.47443/dml.2023.154\n\nAuthors:\n\nPaul Ellis\n\nPaul Ellis\n\n  * This person is not on ResearchGate, or hasn't claimed this research yet.\n\nJason Shi\n\nJason Shi\n\n  * This person is not on ResearchGate, or hasn't claimed this research yet.\n\nThotsaporn Thanatipanonda\n\n  * Mahidol University\n\nAndrew Tu\n\nAndrew Tu\n\n  * This person is not on ResearchGate, or hasn't claimed this research yet.\n\nImage: Request Full-text Paper PDF\n\nRequest full-text PDF\n\nTo read the full-text of this research, you can request a copy directly from the authors.\n\n## No full-text available\n\nImage: Request Full-text Paper PDF\n\nTo read the full-text of this research,\nyou can request a copy directly from the author